  answer: 0,
  explanation: '**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Pertussis causes paroxysmal coughing with inspiratory whoop, but not wheezing. Asthma, bronchiolitis, viral URTI, and foreign body aspiration all cause wheezing.\n\n---\n\n**Pertussis itself is not covered as a clinical disease in the cached lecture material** — *not taken from the course material; the cached decks name Bordetella pertussis only as an age-banded pneumonia pathogen and a vaccine component, with no description of its own cough pattern.* The distinguishing fact the box tests is standard paediatric teaching: pertussis produces paroxysms of forceful coughing followed by a sudden inspiratory gasp (the "whoop"), a cough-dominant illness rather than a lower-airway obstructive one, so it does not produce the musical expiratory wheeze that comes from narrowed, obstructed small airways.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong, i.e. why each does cause wheeze.** *Asthma* is a reversible obstructive airway disease in which bronchospasm and airway inflammation classically produce expiratory wheeze as its defining sign. *RSV bronchiolitis* inflames and obstructs the small airways of infants, producing wheeze alongside crackles as one of its hallmark findings. *Foreign body aspiration* can lodge in a bronchus and cause localised or asymmetric wheeze from the partial obstruction it creates, sometimes with a preceding choking episode. *Viral URTI* can trigger wheeze indirectly, particularly in a child with an underlying reactive airway tendency, or where the "upper" infection in fact extends to involve the lower airway — the deck\'s own focus map notes URTI "may be coincidental with another, more serious illness", which is consistent with a viral URTI accompanying or precipitating a wheezy lower-airway process.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective: 'Identify pertussis as a paroxysmal-cough illness that does not itself cause wheeze, and distinguish it from the genuinely wheeze-causing conditions on the list.',
