  answer: 0,
  explanation: '**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Ampicillin or amoxicillin in EBV causes a characteristic rash.\n\n---\n\n`infectious diseases causing maculopapular rash.txt` gives the mechanism behind this expected outcome: EBV infectious mononucleosis carries a spontaneous rash in only around 10% of cases, but ampicillin or amoxicillin exposure — as here, where the child has already been (mis)treated for tonsillitis — triggers a vivid, itchy maculopapular rash in up to 90% of EBV-infected patients. It is a drug-virus interaction specific to EBV rather than a marker of true penicillin allergy, and does not automatically bar future penicillin use.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Rapid resolution of symptoms* is not what ampicillin does in EBV tonsillitis — ampicillin has no antiviral activity against EBV, so the underlying mononucleosis runs its own course regardless, and the visible effect of the antibiotic here is the rash, not faster recovery. *No adverse effects from ampicillin* is directly contradicted by the well-described EBV-ampicillin rash reaction — this is one of the more reliably reproducible drug reactions in paediatrics, not a neutral non-event. *Increased risk of bacterial superinfection* is not the mechanism at work in this pairing; the rash is a direct drug-virus interaction, not a consequence of secondary bacterial infection following antibiotic use.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
