  explanation: '**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Fever, cough, and descending rash are consistent with measles.\n\n---\n\n`infectious diseases causing maculopapular rash.txt` describes measles\' 2-4 day prodrome as dominated by the three Cs — cough, coryza and conjunctivitis — with a stepwise fever, before a maculopapular rash appears at the hairline and spreads down the body. A severe, prominent cough alongside 3 days of fever and a maculopapular rash is a strong pairing for measles specifically, since a marked respiratory prodrome is one of its distinguishing features against the other exanthems, several of which have little or no cough at all.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Roseola infantum* produces its rash only after the fever has already broken, in an otherwise well-looking child, and does not feature a severe cough. *Rubella* has a mild or absent prodrome in children and no prominent cough; its own signature is lymphadenopathy, not a respiratory illness. *Chickenpox* is a vesicular, intensely itchy eruption, not a maculopapular one, and cough is not part of its classic picture. *Glandular fever* (infectious mononucleosis) causes pharyngitis and lymphadenopathy over a much longer incubation and illness course, with a rash present in only a minority of untreated cases and no characteristic severe cough of its own.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective: 'Recognise the combination of fever, a prominent cough (part of the "three Cs") and a maculopapular rash as measles, and separate it from the other exanthems that lack a significant respiratory prodrome.',
