  answer: 0,
  explanation: '**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Erythema migrans is diagnostic of Lyme disease. Empiric amoxicillin is indicated without waiting for serology.\n\n---\n\n**Lyme disease has no dedicated section in the cached course material** — the only mention anywhere in the theory deck is a single line in the rash-morphology table (secondary lesion shapes) noting that a "Target (iris): concentric rings, a bull\'s-eye" pattern is seen in Lyme disease and erythema multiforme. *The clinical detail below is general medical knowledge, not taken from the course material.* Erythema migrans — the expanding, often bull\'s-eye-shaped ring-like rash this girl has after a woodland exposure (the New Forest is a known UK Lyme-endemic area, with ticks as the vector) — is clinically diagnostic of early Lyme disease on its own. Guidelines recommend starting empirical antibiotics (amoxicillin in a child, or doxycycline if the child is old enough and not tetracycline-sensitive) as soon as this rash is recognised, without waiting for serology, because serology is frequently negative in the first weeks of infection and treatment delay risks progression to disseminated disease. Amoxicillin is the first-line choice for children too young for tetracyclines.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Prescribe an antihistamine* treats itch, not infection, and does nothing for the underlying spirochaetal disease. *Prescribe doxycycline* is a reasonable Lyme antibiotic in an older child or adult, but amoxicillin is generally preferred as first-line in younger children because doxycycline can affect developing teeth; the stem does not establish the girl is old enough for tetracyclines to be preferred over amoxicillin. *Take acute and convalescent Borrelia serology and arrange outpatient follow-up* and *take blood for Borrelia serology* both delay treatment behind a test that is often falsely negative early in the disease — erythema migrans is treated on clinical grounds, not held for serology to confirm it.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective: 'Recognise erythema migrans (a ring-like/bull\'s-eye rash after a tick-exposed outdoor trip) as clinically diagnostic of Lyme disease and treated empirically with amoxicillin without waiting for serology — content not covered in the course material beyond a single rash-morphology mention.',
