  answer: 0,
  explanation: '**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The whole-cell pertussis vaccine is not recommended after 7 years of age due to higher risk of adverse reactions; acellular forms are used instead.\n\n---\n\n*The specific age-7 cutoff for whole-cell pertussis vaccine is not covered in the cached course material and is general vaccine-schedule knowledge, not taken from the course material.* The course deck\'s own vaccine section covers the three vaccine categories and the Egyptian compulsory schedule (whole-cell pertussis given as part of the pentavalent DPT-containing vaccine at 2/4/6 months and boosted at 18 months), but does not spell out an upper age limit for catching up a missed dose — the box\'s reasoning (higher local/systemic reactogenicity of whole-cell pertussis vaccine in older children, with acellular pertussis preferred instead when catch-up vaccination is needed past early childhood) reflects standard practice rather than a line printed in the cached lecture.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Tetanus toxoid*, *Hepatitis B vaccine* and *Inactivated polio vaccine* are all inactivated/toxoid/subunit-type components with no comparable age-related reactogenicity restriction — they remain appropriate, and indeed indicated, for catch-up vaccination in a 10-year-old with missed immunisations, unlike the whole-cell pertussis component.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
